Shane Pearson <shanep@ign.com.au> writes:

> Before I even knew I had the contract, I decided on building
> the firewall around OpenBSD, but I would also like to build
> it with non x86 hardware, as a further step against exploits
> that specifically assume the target CPU to be x86.

I would recommend non-x86 for various other reasons. Right now the
best OpenBSD architectures for building packet forwarding machines are
alpha and powerpc.

> So, my first thought was OpenBSD on the Alpha.
> 
> My priorities are security and reliability, so would you
> recommend Alpha for OpenBSD as a reliable platform or
> something else?

If you choose a decent, modern motherboard - yes.

> Also, if I do go with Alpha, would I require a crypto
> accelerator with a powerful CPU like these? If so are the
> crypto boards supported in the Alpha port?

Yes, yes (afaik).

> I would most likely be looking at using a Samsung board and
> CPU, any issues there?

You mean the up1100 or up2000? I have a up1000 right now and it
works great, it's one of my two main hack machines. The only thing
that's a bit flakey is the IDE. I've never corrupted data, but I pukes a
lot of error messages to the console and downgrades the mode (but the
machine is powerful enough that I barely notice on the performance figures
that I'm running in PIO mode).

I would really love a up2000 to work with, if someone can convince Samsung
to donate one or even sponsor one himself, that would be great.

//art
